{"text": "Florida Department of Office of Executive Director Rick Scott, Governor\n\nLaw Enforcement Post Office Box 1489 Pam Bondi, Attorney General\nTallahassee, Florida 32902-1489 Jeff Atwater, Chief Financial Officer\nGerald M. Bailey (850) 410-7001 \u2018Adam Putnam, Commissioner of Agriculture\nCommissioner www fdle.state.f.us\n\nMarch 13, 2014\n\nMr. Tristram Korten\nEditor\nFlorida Center for Investigative Reporting\nP.O. Box 460068\nFort Lauderdale, FL 33346\n\nDear Mr. Korten:\n\nReference is made to your recent article entitled \u201cState Fingerprint System Flawed, More\nExpensive to Maintain Than To Build,\u201d claiming the department's Automated Fingerprint\nIdentification System (AFIS) is riddled with cost overruns and technical problems. The article is,\nat best, misleading and the associated lawsuit is unfounded.\n\nAFIS is a massive central repository of the state's fingerprint records that includes three\nseparate and unique biometric matching, processing and storage systems: Criminal Tenprint,\nRapid Identification and Retained Civil Applicant Fingerprints. The issues alleged in your article\nrelate primarily to the Criminal Tenprint portion of AFIS. However, your article inaccurately\ncombines the three. Performance and costs associated with the aging Rapid Identification and\nRetained Civil Applicant Fingerprints can in no way be attributed to Criminal Tenprint.\n\nSince Criminal Tenprint became operational in 2009, the number of fingerprint matches by the\ndepartment's crime labs has increased 300 percent and search times have dropped from hours\nor days to just two minutes. It also collects and stores critical palm prints; something the old\nsystem could not do.\n\nThe article suggests Criminal Tenprint is error-prone and implies these deficiencies could cause\na reversal of criminal convictions. However, the system only provides candidate matches;\nactual fingerprint identification is performed manually by trained examiners to avoid false\nidentifications. And the article indicates the system would overlook candidate prints. Fingerprints\nof persons the system is unable to identify are sent to the Federal Bureau of Investigation and\nreturned once an identification is made. Since system implementation, prints identified and\nreturned have decreased to less than one percent of submissions.\n\nPethaps most egregious, the article misleadingly uses narrative from FDLE's recent legislative\nbudget request to conclude the lawsuit is relevant; the two are totally unrelated. Requested\nlegislative funds will remedy stressors the department placed on the system by increasing\nRetained Civil Applicant Fingerprints processing, not because Criminal Tenprint was ill-\nconceived or improperly engineered.\n\nIn summary, Criminal Tenprint was not only\nrequirements, but is performing better than anti\nacross Florida.\n\nilt within budget and in line with contract\nipated. It is a key tool for law enforcement\n\n\u2018Service + Integrity \u00bb Respect \u00bb Quaity",
